SUMMARY:Lecture: Ptolemaic Queens
LOCATION:120 W Gasparilla Plaza Tampa \, FL 33602 United States
DESCRIPTION:Ptolemaic Queens: Women Power in Hellenistic Egypt The Tampa M
 useum of Arts collection includes a limestone portrait of a Ptolemaic prin
 cess\, conventionally identified as Queen Arsinoe II (ca. 318268 )\, from 
 the Joseph Veach Noble Collection. The sculpture provides a fitting introd
 uction to the remarkable women of the Ptolemaic dynasty\, which ruled Egyp
 t for nearly three centuries\, from the death of Alexander the Great to th
 e death of Cleopatra VII (32330 ). Drawing on literary\, archaeological\, 
 and artistic evidence\, this lecture explores how women of the Ptolemaic r
 oyal house could exercise influence and authority in a world shaped by bot
 h Greco-Macedonian and Egyptian traditions of kingship. Particular attenti
 on will be given to four powerful queensArsinoe II\, Berenice II\, Cleopat
 ra I\, and Cleopatra VIIand to the changing political roles they assumed a
 s wives\, mothers\, regents\, co-rulers\, and\, in some cases\, rulers in 
 their own right. The lecture will also examine how royal women were identi
 fied with and worshipped as goddesses\, including Aphrodite\, Demeter\, Ha
 thor\, and Isis. Such religious associations were not merely expressions o
 f piety: they helped articulate ideals of femininity\, fertility\, dynasti
 c continuity\, and royal power\, while contributing to the legitimacy and 
 sacralization of Ptolemaic rule. Lectures are offered free with the cost o
 f daily admission: Art Museum Members : Free Not-Yet-Members : 25 College 
 Students : Free \n\nPermalink: https://www.tampa.gov/node/194696
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.tampa.gov/node/194696
END:VEVENT
